# The layers whose window permits deriving a time slack from a clearance. One row per nameplate
# whose duty cycle leaves the spare spread evenly across the denominator.
#
# The positive form of a negated rule
#   This is the positive form of a rule usually written as a negation, and the positive form is
#   the one that reads. A derived time slack is the clearance, and reading it as demand that
#   survives being held assumes the spare is spread evenly across the denominator: only then is
#   the wait it implies, `q / clearance`, the wait anybody has. A window that is a proper part of
#   the period says it is not (the machine runs 02:00 to 05:00), so the average is over a base that
#   does not exist. `unmeasured` says nobody knows whether it is, which is an assumption wearing a
#   computation's clothes. Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `clearance_is_not_a_duration`.
#
# Two reasons, not two documents
#   So exactly two reasons license the derivation, "it runs the whole period" and "the question
#   has no denominator", across however many layers file one of them. Two answers, not two
#   documents.
#
# The first reason is never read out of an absence
#   The first of the two must not be read out of an absence, which is why this file exists.
#   `window_absent IN ('none', 'notApplicable')` reads `none` as "the duty fraction is one": a
#   number, filed as though it were a nothing. The absence arm carries no size and no origin, so
#   read that way a line that cannot be stopped and a line somebody staffed round the clock
#   license this derivation identically, and neither says so. The licence is read off a filed
#   window, and the two spellings collapse into one.
#
# The period arrives by a semi-join
#   This asks "is there a period equal to this window's unit" and uses no value from the answer,
#   so `EXISTS` states the question it means. It is also a guard. Let `pm.claim.owns` carry an
#   element name rather than a path and `Demand/amount` and `Nameplate/amount` are one `pm:amount`
#   to `units/with_a_period.sqlc`, which then returns two rows per layer: a plain join fans out, a
#   semi-join hides it. `owns` is a path, and the relation is one row per layer.
#
# A whole period is quoted as `1`
#   A whole period is quoted as `1` in the period's own unit, and that is the whole trick. `1 week`
#   against a period of `week` is a duty fraction of one you can read without dividing anything;
#   `5 days` against the same period is a proper part. No unit conversion happens here on
#   purpose: `window_not_applicable_on_a_rate` records what happened when this directory guessed
#   at units in SQL, and a licence decided by a `LIKE` is a reader's judgement wearing a query's
#   clothes.
-- pm:Divisibility/window: absent notApplicable, or filed as one whole period.
SELECT w.filing, w.layer,
       CASE WHEN w.window_absent IS NOT NULL THEN 'the question has no denominator'
            ELSE 'it runs the whole period' END AS licensed_because
FROM (
    :compose(layers/windows.sqlc)
) w
WHERE w.window_absent = 'notApplicable'
   OR (w.window_low = 1 AND w.window_low = w.window_high
       AND EXISTS (
           SELECT 1
           FROM (
               :compose(units/with_a_period.sqlc)
           ) p
           WHERE p.filing = w.filing AND p.layer = w.layer
             AND p.period = w.window_unit
       ))
